Layer groups represent a specialized subset of space groups, characterized by translational symmetry occurring solely along the $c$-axis. Consequently, layer groups offer a suitable framework for modeling material surfaces and interfaces. Although previous efforts have attempted to categorize crystal materials using only layer groups, such approaches are inherently limited because they fail to capture three-dimensional periodicity. In this study, we examine the representation capacity of layer groups by evaluating their ability to classify these eighty collections of nearly identical crystal structures.

