Our findings indicate that variational inference (VI) variational autoencoders (VAEs) successfully learn a representation of layer groups capable of distinguishing among all eighty groups. In contrast, a standard mean field VI VAE struggles to differentiate between the sets of layer groups. Furthermore, hierarchical Bayesian models based on a nested VAE structure also fail to differentiate between the groups when applied to a single collection of layer groups.

